[[:db/retract 17592186357000 :question/answer-fields 17592186357001] {:db/id "q-37131/principal-arcsin-exact-level-set-v1/selection/answer-complete-level-set" :answer/id #uuid "edbdadbd-4697-59bf-985c-9065cd719ac6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z=\\cos(xy),\\quad 0\\le xy\\le\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37131/principal-arcsin-exact-level-set-v1/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ce0ae16-eae7-50e8-8ea5-58dac8da8c7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices [17592186357002 17592186357003 17592186357004 17592186357005 17592186357006 "q-37131/principal-arcsin-exact-level-set-v1/selection/answer-complete-level-set"] :answer-field/correct "q-37131/principal-arcsin-exact-level-set-v1/selection/answer-complete-level-set" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186357000 :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z$ that passes through the point $P(0,1,1).$\n\nGive the full level set, including the necessary range restriction from the principal real arcsine.\n\nHint: Compute $f(0,1,1)$, then use the principal range $-\\pi/2\\le\\arcsin z\\le\\pi/2$ before taking sine." :question/worked-solution "The level surface through $P=(0,1,1)$ has level constant\n$$c=f(0,1,1)=0+\\arcsin(1)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$$\nThus $xy+\\arcsin z=\\pi/2$, or $\\arcsin z=\\pi/2-xy$. The principal real arcsine takes values in $[-\\pi/2,\\pi/2]$. Therefore\n$$-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\frac{\\pi}{2}-xy\\le\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad 0\\le xy\\le\\pi.$$\nTaking sine gives $z=\\sin(\\pi/2-xy)=\\cos(xy)$. Conversely, if $0\\le xy\\le\\pi$ and $z=\\cos(xy)$, then $\\pi/2-xy$ lies in the principal arcsine range, so $\\arcsin z=\\pi/2-xy$ and $f(x,y,z)=\\pi/2$. The exact full level set is\n$$\\boxed{\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3:z=\\cos(xy),\\ 0\\le xy\\le\\pi\\}}.$$\nThe range restriction is essential. For instance $(x,y,z)=(2\\pi,1,1)$ satisfies $z=\\cos(xy)$, but $f(2\\pi,1,1)=2\\pi+\\pi/2=5\\pi/2$, not $\\pi/2$. The source's revealed answer a provides the algebraic relation but omits the restriction. Its original prompt, options, source grade and solution remain preserved; the study choice supplies the complete level set without changing the original function or point." :question/answer-fields ["q-37131/principal-arcsin-exact-level-set-v1/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
